{"paper_id": "paper-899bac2a83e04655", "doi": "10.1002/mbo3.70362", "title": "One Health Insights From Pteropus medius: Nipah Virus Spillover, Microbiota, and Antimicrobial Resistance.", "analysis": {"analysis": {"scope_and_question": "This review examines the relationship among Nipah virus (NiV) spillover, Pteropus medius bat microbiota, and antimicrobial resistance (AMR) within a One Health framework, focusing on zoonotic transmission pathways and environmental factors in Bangladesh.", "evidence_base_and_review_method": "A narrative literature review was conducted using PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, ScienceDirect, and Google Scholar to identify relevant peer-reviewed studies published between 2006 and 2025.", "consensus_and_key_conclusions": "Pteropus medius bats harbor clinically important resistant bacteria, including ESBL-producing Escherichia coli, Salmonella spp., and methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus spp. Habitat fragmentation, wastewater contamination, urbanization, agricultural intensification, and increased human-wildlife interactions are major drivers facilitating both NiV spillover and AMR dissemination.", "controversies_and_evidence_gaps": "The review identifies bidirectional exchange of antimicrobial resistance genes (ARGs) among wildlife, livestock, and humans in bat roosts near agricultural lands, wastewater discharge sites, and peri-urban settlements, though specific mechanisms and quantitative data remain limited.", "research_and_practice_implications": "Integrated One Health surveillance and environmental management strategies are recommended to mitigate future zoonotic and AMR threats in Bangladesh, emphasizing the need for targeted interventions in high-risk interfaces."}, "summary_en": "This narrative review examines the interconnected risks of Nipah virus spillover and antimicrobial resistance (AMR) from Pteropus medius bats within a One Health framework. The study identifies bats as reservoirs for both NiV and clinically important resistant bacteria, with habitat fragmentation, wastewater contamination, and urbanization driving both spillover and AMR dissemination. Evidence suggests bidirectional exchange of resistance genes among wildlife, livestock, and humans in peri-urban interfaces. The review underscores the importance of integrated surveillance and environmental management to mitigate these dual threats in Bangladesh.", "evidence_ids": {"scope_and_question": ["A1", "A4"], "evidence_base_and_review_method": ["A5"], "consensus_and_key_conclusions": ["A6", "A8", "A9"], "controversies_and_evidence_gaps": ["A7"], "research_and_practice_implications": ["A10"]}, "confidence": "low", "postprocess_audit": {"removed_duplicate_sentences": 0, "repaired_incomplete_fields": [], "evidence_replacements": [], "policy": "v10-exclusive-rhetorical-fields-1"}, "language_contract": {"policy_version": "v17.1-source-language-safe-english-1", "source_language": "en", "verified_english": true, "replacements": [], "original_evidence_preserved": false}, "status": "passed", "kind": "review", "provider": "bigmodel", "model": "glm-4.5-air", "attempts": [{"task": "paper_review_analysis", "provider": "gemini", "model": "", "at": "2026-07-27T20:51:05+00:00", "status": "skipped", "failure_category": "authentication_failed", "error": "authentication_failed"}, {"task": "paper_review_analysis", "provider": "bigmodel", "model": "glm-4.5", "at": "2026-07-27T20:51:05+00:00", "status": "skipped", "failure_category": "model_cooldown", "error": "model is temporarily cooling down"}, {"task": "paper_review_analysis", "provider": "bigmodel", "model": "glm-4.5-air", "retry_index": 0, "at": "2026-07-27T20:51:05+00:00", "system_chars": 4668, "prompt_chars": 7813, "status": "success", "response_model": "glm-4.5-air", "elapsed_ms": 5204, "parser_audit": {"policy_version": "v17.2-flexible-json-parser-1", "method": "direct_json", "response_chars": 2433, "repaired": false, "truncated_signal": false}, "normalization_audit": {"policy_version": "v