{"paper_id": "paper-a9f4e77fb565497f", "doi": "10.1080/22221751.2026.2703394", "title": "Longitudinal in vivo imaging reveals asynchronous, incomplete Nipah virus clearance with prolonged focal CNS involvement in IFNAR-/- mice.", "analysis": {"analysis": {"research_question_and_background": "Nipah virus (NiV) infection causes fatal acute respiratory and neurological disease, and survivors may develop long-term sequelae or experience relapsing encephalitis months to years after recovery. The basis of divergent disease trajectories and incomplete viral clearance remains poorly defined.", "study_design_and_population": "We established a red-shifted bioluminescent resonance energy transfer reporter system to enable longitudinal tracking of NiV infection in vivo and applied it in mouse models of infection.", "methods": "We integrated longitudinal in vivo imaging with RT-qPCR, histopathology, and in situ hybridization to study NiV infection in IFNAR-/- mice, examining route-, strain-, and immune-dependent differences in viral dissemination and clearance.", "main_results": "Intranasal infection remained largely confined to the respiratory tract, while intraperitoneal infection resulted in systemic spread followed by prolonged, fluctuating focal viral signal detectable up to 42 days post-infection. Persistent viral RNA was detected in discrete brain regions after apparent clinical recovery, indicating anatomically restricted persistence.", "interpretation_and_novelty": "The findings reveal the spatiotemporal dynamics of incomplete NiV clearance in vivo and provide a framework to study relapsing disease, demonstrating asynchronous and focal CNS involvement.", "scientific_and_public_health_significance": "These results improve understanding of NiV persistence and relapsing encephalitis, informing surveillance strategies and therapeutic approaches targeting anatomically restricted viral reservoirs.", "limitations_and_evidence_strength": "The study was conducted in IFNAR-/- mice, which may not fully recapitulate human immune responses; however, the longitudinal imaging approach provides high-confidence evidence of viral dynamics."}, "summary_en": "This study investigated Nipah virus (NiV) clearance dynamics using longitudinal in vivo imaging in IFNAR-/- mice. The researchers developed a bioluminescent reporter system to track infection over time, revealing that intraperitoneal infection leads to systemic spread and prolonged focal viral signal in the CNS, detectable up to 42 days post-infection. Despite apparent clinical recovery, persistent viral RNA was found in discrete brain regions, indicating anatomically restricted persistence. The findings demonstrate asynchronous and incomplete viral clearance, providing insights into the mechanisms of relapsing encephalitis in survivors. This research offers a framework for studying relapsing disease and informs strategies for surveillance and targeted therapies.", "evidence_ids": {"research_question_and_background": ["A1", "A2", "A3"], "study_design_and_population": ["A4"], "methods": ["A4", "A5"], "main_results": ["A6", "A7"], "interpretation_and_novelty": ["A5", "A8"], "scientific_and_public_health_significance": ["A8"], "limitations_and_evidence_strength": ["A7"]}, "confidence": "high", "postprocess_audit": {"removed_duplicate_sentences": 0, "repaired_incomplete_fields": [], "evidence_replacements": [], "policy": "v10-exclusive-rhetorical-fields-1"}, "language_contract": {"policy_version": "v17.1-source-language-safe-english-1", "source_language": "en", "verified_english": true, "replacements": [], "original_evidence_preserved": false}, "status": "passed", "kind": "research", "provider": "bigmodel", "model": "glm-4.5-air", "attempts": [{"task": "paper_research_analysis", "provider": "gemini", "model": "", "at": "2026-07-27T20:51:21+00:00", "status": "skipped", "failure_category": "authentication_failed", "error": "authentication_failed"}, {"task": "paper_research_analysis", "provider": "bigm