{"paper_id": "paper-6e3e219b94c2a787", "doi": "10.3389/fbinf.2026.1846380", "title": "Computational design of immunogenic peptide–ligand conjugates for targeted therapy against Nipah virus infection", "analysis": {"analysis": {"research_question_and_background": "Nipah virus (NiV) poses a major risk to global public health due to its high infectivity and associated mortality rates, and currently no licensed vaccines or antiviral medications are available for NiV infection.", "study_design_and_population": "This study employed computational methods to design a peptide-ligand conjugate (PLC) immunotherapeutic approach targeting NiV-infected cells, leveraging pre-existing immune responses in NiV-endemic populations.", "methods": "We employed biomolecular modeling (in silico) to establish binding affinities and perform docking studies using a compound library from the MolProphet database, with a non-cleavable oxime linker selected for enhanced stability and conjugation.", "main_results": "The PLC design demonstrated selective binding capacity to a transmembrane protein present on NiV-infected cells, with the oxime linker providing enhanced stability and the peptide epitope design incorporating regions associated with established long-term immunity.", "interpretation_and_novelty": "This PLC system represents a promising framework for antiviral therapeutic development by harnessing pre-existing immune recognition to promote selective clearance of NiV-infected cells, highlighting critical structural components and functional roles of PLCs in therapeutic development.", "scientific_and_public_health_significance": "The findings provide insights into drug target screening and rational design strategies for enhancing targeting specificity and molecular stability of immunotherapeutics against NiV infection, with future work needed for experimental validation and efficacy studies.", "limitations_and_evidence_strength": "The evidence strength is moderate as the study relies solely on computational modeling without experimental validation, limiting confidence in the actual therapeutic efficacy of the proposed PLC design."}, "summary_en": "This study addresses the urgent need for Nipah virus (NiV) therapeutics by computationally designing a peptide-ligand conjugate (PLC) immunotherapeutic approach. The PLC selectively targets NiV-infected cells by exploiting pre-existing immune responses in endemic populations, using a non-cleavable oxime linker for stability and immunogenic epitopes from measles, mumps, and rubella vaccines. Computational modeling confirmed selective binding to a transmembrane protein on infected cells and enhanced stability. While the findings offer a promising framework for antiviral development, the evidence strength is moderate due to the lack of experimental validation. Future work should focus on in vitro and in vivo efficacy studies to confirm the therapeutic potential of this approach.", "evidence_ids": {"research_question_and_background": ["A1", "A2"], "study_design_and_population": ["A4"], "methods": ["A5", "A6", "A7"], "main_results": ["A8", "A9"], "interpretation_and_novelty": ["A10", "A11"], "scientific_and_public_health_significance": ["A11", "A12"], "limitations_and_evidence_strength": ["A12"]}, "confidence": "low", "postprocess_audit": {"removed_duplicate_sentences": 0, "repaired_incomplete_fields": [], "evidence_replacements": [], "policy": "v10-exclusive-rhetorical-fields-1"}, "language_contract": {"policy_version": "v17.1-source-language-safe-english-1", "source_language": "en", "verified_english": true, "replacements": [], "original_evidence_preserved": false}, "status": "passed", "kind": "research", "provider": "bigmodel", "model": "glm-4.5-air", "attempts": [{"task": "paper_research_analysis", "provider": "gemini", "model": "", "at": "2026-07-27T20:52:25+00:00", "status": "skipped", "failure_category": "authentication_failed", "error": "authentication_failed"}, {"task": "paper_research_analysis", "provi